creasing yield strength while reducing transition temperature through progressively lower finishing temperatures in the α-region. [Data: Entities (1047); Relationships (1482, 1483, 1276)]\\n\\nThe process is therefore connected to phase-specific deformation and to the strength response of the final steel. The γ + α region is specifically linked to increasing yield strength as deformation increases. [Data: Relationships (690, 1483)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Finishing temperature and α-region processing are key strengthening levers\",\"explanation\":\"Within the α-region, lowering the finishing temperature increases yield strength while reducing transition temperature. The data associate this behavior with controlled or continuum rolling and with development of a beneficial ferritic substructure. [Data: Entities (239, 1047, 536); Relationships (1276)]\\n\\nThis relationship identifies α-region finishing conditions as an important processing control for balancing strength and transition-temperature behavior. The evidence describes the direction of the effect but does not specify quantitative limits or an optimum temperature. [Data: Entities (239, 1047); Relationships (1276)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Grain refinement is represented through deformation and effective grain size\",\"explanation\":\"Continuum rolling uses heavy, repeated deformations to refine both γ and α grains. Grain refinement is also represented mathematically through the effective grain-size term in the yield-strength relation, \\u0000σy = σ0 + k de−1/2, indicating that effective grain size is a modeled contributor to yield strength. [Data: Entities (1047, 1076, 783, 803); Relationships (1507, 1023, 1047)]\\n\\nThe rewritten equation explicitly connects the original relation to effective grain size, while the processing relationships connect repeated deformation to grain refinement. Together, these records provide both a process-level and equation-level account of grain-size strengthening. [Data: Entities (783, 803); Relationships (1023, 1047)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Alloy content and precipitation strengthening provide additional strength contributions\",\"explanation\":\"The community links alloy content to increases in yield strength through grain refinement and precipitation strengthening. Precipitation strengthening is described as especially important when niobium and vanadium precipitation progresses during holding at coiling temperature, producing a marked increase in yield strength. [Data: Entities (536, 878, 884); Relationships (1173, 1174)]\\n\\nThese records show that composition and thermal history are related to the strengthening response, alongside deformation processing. The provided data establish the mechanisms and associations but do not provide specific alloy percentages or coiling-temperature schedules. [Data: Entities (536, 878, 884)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Cell structure is included as a modeled yield-strength contribution\",\"explanation\":\"Cell contribution is represented by the term kc dc−1/2 fc in equation (11), where cell size and cell fraction affect the modeled yield strength. This places substructure or cell structure alongside grain size, alloying, and precipitation as a distinct strengthening contribution. [Data: Entities (801, 536); Relationships (1044)]\\n\\nThe available evidence indicates that the cell contribution may increase or decrease the modeled yield strength depending on the relevant cell-size and cell-fraction terms. It does not provide numerical values for those parameters or a quantified net effect. [Data: Entities (801); Relationships (1044)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The Bauschinger effect introduces a countervailing strength loss\",\"explanation\":\"The Bauschinger effect is described as causing a loss in yield strength and as having a pronounced influence on discontinuous yielding in polygonal steels. Polygonal steels are specifically associated with discontinuous yielding, distinguishing this behavior from the continuous yielding described 